        How to Start a Successful Daycare in California
      </h1>
      <p style="color:#86868b;font-size:0.9rem;margin-bottom:28px;">
        June 2, 2026 · 8 min read
      </p>

      <p>
        Opening a daycare in California is a meaningful way to serve families in
        your community — but it also comes with clear licensing, safety, and
        training requirements. Whether you plan to run a small family childcare
        home or grow into a larger center, success starts with understanding the
        rules and building a safe, compliant program from day one.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Choose your program type</h2>
      <p>
        California distinguishes between family childcare homes (typically in a
        provider's residence) and childcare centers (commercial or dedicated
        facilities). Capacity, staffing, and facility rules differ. Most new
        providers begin with a family childcare home because startup costs are
        lower and the path to licensing is more approachable — but you should
        choose based on your space, neighborhood demand, and long-term goals.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Understand licensing through Community Care Licensing</h2>
      <p>
        All licensed childcare in California is overseen by the Community Care
        Licensing Division (CCL) of the Department of Social Services. Before
        you accept children, you must complete orientation, submit an
        application, pass background checks, and receive a facility inspection.
        CCL publishes clear checklists for home and center applicants — use them
        as your roadmap rather than guessing what an inspector will look for.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Attend the required licensing orientation for your program type</li>
        <li>Complete Live Scan fingerprinting for all adults in the home or center</li>
        <li>Prepare your facility to meet health, safety, and ratio requirements</li>
        <li>Maintain written policies for illness, emergencies, and parent communication</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>3. Complete EMSA Preventive Health & Safety training</h2>
      <p>
        California requires childcare providers to complete EMSA-approved
        Preventive Health and Safety training, including nutrition and lead
        poisoning prevention components. This 8-hour course covers illness
        prevention, safe sleep, injury prevention, nutrition, and emergency
        preparedness — the foundation of a safe program. At Alert Training
        Services, we offer convenient online evening sessions so you can complete
        this requirement while preparing your home or center.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/preventative-health-and-safety-training" style="color:#E02B2B;font-weight:600;"
          >View upcoming Preventive Health & Safety classes →</a
        >
      </p>

      <h2>4. Set up your space and safety systems</h2>
      <p>
        Inspectors and parents alike want to see that your environment is
        intentionally designed for young children. That means gated stairs,
        covered outlets, age-appropriate equipment, clear evacuation routes, a
        working first-aid kit, and documented emergency contacts. Create a daily
        routine that includes supervision plans, handwashing, and safe food
        handling — consistency builds trust with families and keeps children
        safe.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Build your business foundation</h2>
      <p>
        Beyond licensing, treat your daycare like a small business. Define your
        enrollment capacity, tuition rates, hours of operation, and contract
        terms. Register with your city or county if a business license is
        required, consult a tax professional about reporting income, and
        consider liability insurance designed for childcare providers. Strong
        policies upfront prevent misunderstandings later.
      </p>

      <h2>6. Market thoughtfully and enroll gradually</h2>
      <p>
        Start with a realistic enrollment goal — filling one or two spots
        reliably is better than overpromising. Ask satisfied families for
        referrals, list your program on local parent networks, and keep your
        communication professional from the first inquiry. Your reputation
        grows through consistent safety, warmth, and reliability.
      </p>

